interface S { String r(); }
class Base implements S { public String r() { return "x"; } }
class Trim implements S {
    final S in; Trim(S in) { this.in = in; }
    public String r() { return in.r().substring(1); }
}
class Wrap implements S {
    final S in; Wrap(S in) { this.in = in; }
    public String r() { return "[" + in.r() + "]"; }
}
System.out.println(new Trim(new Wrap(new Base())).r());
System.out.println(new Wrap(new Trim(new Base())).r());
The output is x] then []. The innermost runs first. In the first, Wrap gives [x] and Trim removes the first character. In the second, Trim gives an empty string and Wrap brackets it. Decorator order changes the result.

| Composite | Decorator
Wraps | Many children (a tree) | Exactly one component
Purpose | Treat a group like a single item | Add behaviour to one object
A call does | Forwards to every child and combines the results | Forwards to the wrapped object, adding work before or after
Examples | Folders and files; AWT Container; Spring CompositeCacheManager | BufferedInputStream; Collections.unmodifiableList
interface Item { BigDecimal price(); }
record Box(List<Item> items) implements Item {          // Composite
    public BigDecimal price() {
        return items.stream().map(Item::price).reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
    }
}
record GiftWrapped(Item inner) implements Item {         // Decorator
    public BigDecimal price() { return inner.price().add(new BigDecimal("2.50")); }
}
Rule of thumb: count what is inside. Many parts that add up to a whole is Composite; one object with an extra layer is Decorator.
